\begin_layout Standard
One of the cornerstones of geometry is the problem of packing circles and
 spheres into a container, e.g., a square or a hypercube.
 It dates back to the 17th century when Kepler conjectured his famous bound
 on the average density of any packing of spheres in the three-dimensional
 Euclidean space
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kepler2010six"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The problem has been investigated for example by Lagrange [?] who solved
 it in the setting of two dimensions, by Hales and Ferguson
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "hales2006formulation"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 who proved Kepler's original conjecture, and by Viazovska
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "viazovska2017sphere"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 who studied the problem in dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $8$
\end_inset

 and who was awared the fields medal in 2022 for her work.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Circle packing is the study of the arrangement of non-overlapping circles
 (of equal or varying sizes) within a containing space.
 In higher dimensions, the problem is known as sphere packing.
 The history of sphere packing goes back to the 17th century.
 Kepler
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kepler2010six"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 conjectured in 1611, a bound on the average density of any packing of spheres
 in the three-dimensional Euclidean space.
 In 1773, Lagrange proved that in the two-dimensional Euclidean plane, the
 highest-density lattice packing of circles is the hexagonal packing arrangement
, in which the centers of the circles are arranged in a hexagonal lattice,
 and each circle is surrounded by six other circles.
 Kepler's conjecture remained open for centuries, until recently in 2006,
 Hales and Ferguson
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "hales2006formulation"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 presented a formal proof in the affirmative.
 Sphere packing and arrangements of spheres have eluded mathematicians over
 the centuries (see the books on sphere packing
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "aste2001pursuit, fejes1942dichteste, rogers1958packing, stephenson2005introduction"
literal "false"

\end_inset

), and still considered to be one of the cornerstone areas of research in
 Geometry.
 In a recent breakthrough, Viazovska
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "viazovska2017sphere"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 studied the sphere packing in dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $8$
\end_inset

, for which she was awarded the field medal in 2022.
 The work has been later extended to dimension
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $24$
\end_inset

 by Cohn et al.
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "cohn2017sphere"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

A natural corresponding optimization question is the 
\emph on
geometric knapsack problem
\emph default
, where we are given a set of 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

-dimensional objects for some 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

, e.g., circles or (hyper-)spheres, but possibly also other shapes like squares,
 cubes, pentagons, hexagons, etc., each of them having a given profit.
 The goal is to find the subset of maximum total profit that can be packed
 non-overlappingly into a given square or (hyper-)cube.
 
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
this work, we primarily focus on circles and hyperspheres in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

, fat convex polygons in the plane in which each angle is strictly larger
 than 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\pi}{2}$
\end_inset

 (hence, this generalizes regular 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

-gons for 
\begin_inset Formula $k\ge5$
\end_inset

), and arbitrary fat convex objects that are by a factor of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Theta(\varepsilon)$
\end_inset

 smaller than the knapsack.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

