\begin_layout Standard
Geometric knapsack is known to be 
\family sans
NP
\family default
-hard, already for 
\begin_inset Formula $d=2$
\end_inset

 and if all input objects are squares or disks 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "demaine-Origami-NPHard"
literal "false"

\end_inset

[hardness for squares].
 This motivates designing approximation algorithms for the problem.
 For hypercubes in any fixed constant dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

, there is a polynomial time 
\begin_inset Formula $(1+\epsilon)$
\end_inset

-approximation algorithm known for any constant 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

, i.e., a polynomial time approximation scheme (PTAS).
 Thus, this is the best possible approximation guarantee we can achieve,
 unless 
\family sans
P
\family default
=
\family sans
NP
\family default
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
However, for other classes of objects we are far from this level of understandin
g, even if we consider only fat convex objects.
 For circles, the best known result is a 
\begin_inset Formula $(3+\epsilon)$
\end_inset

-approximation [?] (but the problem admits a PTAS under resource augmentation
 in any constant dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

, i.e., if we can slightly increase the size of the knapsack by a factor of
 
\begin_inset Formula $1+\epsilon$
\end_inset

 in one dimension [WAOA]).
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
but it is well possible that the problem admits a PTAS.
 Note that for circles and hyperspheres in any constant dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

, there is a PTAS under resource augmentation, meaning that we can slightly
 increase the size of the knapsack by a factor of 
\begin_inset Formula $1+\epsilon$
\end_inset

 in one dimension [WAOA].
\end_layout

\end_inset

 There is an algorithm for triangles whose approximation ratio is a not
 explicitly specified constant [ICALP-Merino-Wiese] (and it is required
 that we can rotate the triangles arbitrarily).
 It is easy to obtain approximation ratios of 
\begin_inset Formula $O(2^{d})$
\end_inset

 for fat objects in any dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

 via volume arguments [citation?].
 However, we are not aware of any more specific results for, e.g., pentagons
 or hexagons
\begin_inset Note Note
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
, or fat polytopes in dimensions 
\begin_inset Formula $d\ge3$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 This raises the following question.
 
