\begin_layout Standard
In this paper, we answer our question above for a wide class of geometric
 objects.
 Our first result is that for 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

-dimensional spheres in any constant dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

, for any 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

 we can compute in polynomial time a set of spheres that can be packed non-overl
appingly inside the given knapsack and whose profit is at least 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{1+\epsilon}\mathrm{OPT}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathrm{OPT}$
\end_inset

 denotes the profit of the optimal solution.
 Moreover, for all but at most 
\begin_inset Formula $O_{\varepsilon}(1)$
\end_inset

 spheres our algorithm outputs the precise coordinates in the packing, for
 the other 
\begin_inset Formula $O_{\varepsilon}(1)$
\end_inset

 spheres it outputs them up to an only exponentially small error in each
 dimension.
 Note that it is not clear that there always exists a 
\begin_inset Formula $(1+\epsilon)$
\end_inset

-approximate solution in which all coordinates are rational and need only
 a polynomial number of bits.
 However, assuming that this is the case, we show that we can find such
 coordinates in polynomial time.
 We remark that there are related packing problems for which it is known
 that irrational coordinates are sometimes necessary and that computing
 them is 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

-complete (and hence possibly even harder than 
\family sans
NP
\family default
-hard) [??].
 On the other hand, note that we guarantee that our computed set of spheres
 fits into the given knapsack (without resource augmentation).
 
\begin_inset Note Note
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
In particular, we compute the value of the optimal solution up to a factor
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $1+\epsilon$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To compute our packing, we first enumerate over the large spheres in the
 optimal solution, i.e.
 the spheres whose radius is at least a constant fraction of the side length
 of the knapsack.
 Also, we guess their placement up to a polynomially small error which yields
 a small range of possible placements for each of them.
 We would like to place small spheres into the remaining part of the knapsack.
 Unfortunately, we do not know precisely which part of the knapsack is available
 for them since we know the precise placement of the large spheres only
 approximately.
 Thus, there is some area of the knapsack that is 
\emph on
maybe
\emph default
 used by the large spheres; however, potentially the optimal solution uses
 it for placing small spheres.
 Our key insight is that this area is small compared to the area that is
 for sure 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 used by large spheres in the optimal solution.
 Using that our objects are spheres, we show that there is some area in
 each corner of the knapsack that cannot be covered by any large sphere
 in any solution and whose size is at least a constant fraction of the knapsack
 (see Figure
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

??).
 We use this area to compensate the fact that we do not know the precise
 placement of our large spheres, and waste some space because of this.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When we select and pack the small spheres, we define a constant number of
 (small) identical knapsacks that fit into the given knapsack together with
 the large spheres, and into into which we place our small spheres.
 For this task, we argue that it is sufficient to have an algorithm that
 uses resource augmentation, i.e., that increases the size of each bin by
 a factor of 
\begin_inset Formula $1+\epsilon$
\end_inset

 (in each dimension).
 This problem can be solved via known techniques [?].
 Thus, on a high level, we reduce the problem of packing arbitrary spheres
 into one knapsack to the problem of packing small spheres into a constant
 number of knapsacks under resource augmentation.
\end_layout

