\begin_layout Plain Layout
Geometric packing, and in particular, sphere packing is an extremely challenging
 mathematical problem, due to the combinatorial hardness that primarily
 comes from the complications of dealing with irrational coordinates.
 Evidently, the long-standing open conjecture by Erdős and Oler
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "oler1961finite"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 from 1961, regarding the optimal packings of unit circles into an equilateral
 triangle has only been proven up to 
\begin_inset Formula $15$
\end_inset

 disks.
 There have been numerous attempts to understand the structural properties
 of packing circles and spheres.
 See
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "fekete2023packing, fodor2003densest, goldberg1971packing, hifi2009literature"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for an overview of these works.
\end_layout

\end_inset

For geometric knapsack for axis-parallel rectangles (i.e., when 
\begin_inset Formula $d=2$
\end_inset

), the best polynomial time algorithm has an approximation ratio of 
\begin_inset Formula $17/9+\epsilon$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "galvez2017approximating"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 and there is a pseudo-polynomial time algorithm with a ratio of 
\begin_inset Formula $4/3+\epsilon$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "galvez2021improved"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 If it is allowed to rotate the rectangles by 90 degrees, there is also
 a polynomial time 
\begin_inset Formula $(1.5+\epsilon)$
\end_inset

-approximation algorithm known
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "galvez2017approximating"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Moreover, the problem admits a QPTAS if the input data are quasi-polynomially
 bounded integers
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "adamaszek2014quasi"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 For the setting of packing circles, there is a PTAS under resource augmentation
 in one dimension, assuming that the profit of each circle equals its area,
 due to Lintzmayer, Miyazawa, and Xavier
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lintzmayer2018two"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 This was improved to the above mentioned PTAS under resource augmentation
 in one dimension for spheres with arbitrarily profits in any constant dimension
 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

, due to Chagas, Dell’Arriva, and Miyazawa
