 For example, for the settings of squares or (hyper-)cubes, the problem
 admits an asymptotic PTAS
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bansal2006bin"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 In the case of rectangles, the best known result is an asymptotic 
\begin_inset Formula $1.405$
\end_inset

-approximation
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bansal2014improved"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 but an asymptotic PTAS cannot exist, unless 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathsf{P=NP}$
